				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/electrix" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6191880); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6191880&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606942354" alt="Electrix" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6191880)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/electrix" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6191880); return false;" >Electrix</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606667161 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">29 Nov 2020 16:26</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4831420">
									

<p>(Requested to take another look at this through PM)</p>
<p>Alright, some things are definitely better:</p>
<ul>
<li>The exploration log is now written as an exploration log, which is great!</li>
<li>The Special Containment Procedures section is more structured</li>
<li>Tone has improved in the Description section</li>
<li>There are less grammatical errors and sentence structure has been improved, which makes it easier to read (however if you want to have the whole article checked for these kinds of things, you may want to find someone more knowledgeable in that field than me)</li>
</ul>
<p>It's alright thus far, but I've noticed a very important thing: The tone in the exploration log and other documents is very unprofessional</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Crit&nbsp;on&nbsp;tone</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Alright, let's take a quote:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Researcher</strong>: Have you experienced anything yet class-D? Your heart rate is slowly gaining pace.<br />
<strong>D-45305</strong>: Yea, I'm fine. It's just… I don't want to do this anymore. Please don't make me do this, I'm very claustro-<br />
<strong>Researcher</strong>: Hush now, you know exactly why you are here. Don't forget it. You will comply with this mission and orders and you'll not complain. Do I make myself clear?<br />
<strong>D-45305</strong>: (Silence)<br />
<strong>Researcher</strong>: Do I make myself clear.<br />
<strong>D-45305</strong>: Yes. <em>sniff</em><br />
<strong>Researcher</strong>: Good rat.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If a researcher handles a D-class in this way, they can expect a lengthy disciplinary hearing from the ethics committee. Why? Because D-class are still people, even if they are used for experiments. They aren't meant to be expendable, contrary to popular belief; D-class just means that they don't have the clearance codes for almost all files and places. Calling a test-subject, even an unwilling one, ''rat'' is highly unprofessional and unexpected from a trained Foundation researcher. It should be noted that this does not apply to the d-class themselves.</p>
<p>Also, I've noticed that you write the researcher like some evil maniac: he's intimidating the D-class, he's saying that the D-class isn't allowed to feel happy, etc. You should understand this: <strong>The Foundation is not evil</strong>. Even if some of their decisions are questionable, they won't abuse, hurt, or destroy unnecessarily. They even have an ethics committee to ensure that this happens. Try to keep this in mind when writing the character of the researcher.</p>
<p><strong>TLDR</strong>; The Foundation is not evil, but cold and calculated. Researchers don't intimidate D-class and remain professional when conducting experiments or supervising explorations.</p>
<p>If you're still willing to go with an assertive researcher, I have a quick example with the previous quote:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Researcher</strong>: Have you experienced anything yet class-D? Your heart rate is slowly gaining pace.<br />
<strong>D-45305</strong>: Yea, I'm fine. It's just… I don't want to do this anymore. Please don't make me do this, I'm very claustro-<br />
<strong>Researcher</strong>: D-45305, please continue down the tunnel. Failure to comply will increase your sentence.<br />
<em>(silence)</em><br />
<strong>Researcher</strong>: D-45305, do you understand?<br />
<strong>D-45305</strong>: Yes.<br />
<strong>Researcher</strong>: Good. Please continue down the tunnel.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Something like that. Please keep this in mind when revising your exploration log.</p>
<p><em>P.S.</em> Here's a quote, again:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Both personnel were hypnotized at the sight of SCP-XXXX-A-2 sky which represented a neubla. The sky consistently changed in color and gases creating a mesmerizing pattern to the beat of the current song playing in SCP-XXXX-A-2. It appears to have a psychological affect causing all creatures that observe it to feel relaxed and emotional.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If a property of an SCP is discovered in an exploration log, it is best to make these kinds of observations at the end of the log. You only write the things that actually happen in the exploration; it's usually based on a recording, and a recording usually doesn't tell you that someone was mesmerized or that the SCP seemed to have a special effect on them. You can make these kinds of remarks as footnotes, or as a small section after the log titled ''note'' or something like that.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>That is all! (for now) If you want me to look at something specific, just DM me.</p>

